-- Passage rg11-main-idea-summary-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('cf6ed342-15bb-50cf-9eb3-94e012db2791', 'rg11-main-idea-summary-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Letting the Tide Back In$q$, $q$For most of the last century, the low fields along the fictional Calder Bay were farmland protected by an earthen wall. The wall, built by hand generations ago, kept the tides out so that farmers could graze cattle on land that had once been salt marsh. By the time the last farm family sold its land to the regional parks office, the wall was crumbling, and repairing it would have cost more than the fields were worth.

Planners chose a different approach. Instead of rebuilding the wall, they cut three wide gaps in it and let the sea return. Within two years, tidal water flowed in and out twice a day through a network of new channels. Salt-tolerant grasses, which had survived in thin strips along the old wall, spread across the flats.

The returning marsh did more than change the view. Marshes slow incoming water, and the dense grasses trap sediment carried by the tide. Over time, this sediment raises the level of the marsh surface, which helps it keep pace with gradually rising seas. Surveys at Calder Bay found that the restored flats gained height every year, while a nearby concrete seawall required constant repair.

Wildlife followed the water. Wading birds that had not nested in the area for decades began feeding in the shallow channels, and young fish used the sheltered pools as nurseries before moving out into the open bay. Local fishing crews reported larger catches of several species within a few seasons.

The project had costs. Some neighbors missed the open pastures, and a footpath along the wall had to be moved inland. Still, Calder Bay has become a model that coastal planners from other regions visit. It suggests that, in the right places, stepping back and allowing a natural system to recover can protect a shoreline as well as, or better than, building ever higher walls.$q$, 'draft')
