   $q$How do the passage's ideas about shoreline protection and wildlife relate to each other?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Both result from the single choice to let the tide return, and together they build the case that restoration has several benefits.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Wildlife is the main idea, and shoreline protection is mentioned only as a minor side effect that planners did not expect.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-emphasis"},{"text":"Shoreline protection caused the wildlife to return, because the birds came back only after the concrete seawall was repaired.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confuses-cause-effect"},{"text":"They compete with each other, since the sediment that protects the shore also fills the pools that young fish need.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Both result from the single choice to let the tide return, and together they build the case that restoration has several benefits.$q$, $q$Paragraphs 3 and 4 each describe a benefit of the returning marsh, and paragraph 5 draws on both to present Calder Bay as a model. The passage never says sediment harms the fish nurseries.$q$, 'draft', 'cf6ed342-15bb-50cf-9eb3-94e012db2791')
