-- Passage rg11-main-idea-summary-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('26452a93-5902-5bad-acae-1157580c1e77', 'rg11-main-idea-summary-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Why Bridges Have Gaps$q$, $q$Drive across a long highway bridge and you may feel a soft thump every few hundred feet as your tires roll over a metal strip set into the road. Those strips cover expansion joints, gaps that engineers leave on purpose. Without them, the bridge would slowly tear itself apart.

The problem is temperature. Steel and concrete expand as they warm and contract as they cool. The change in any single foot of material is tiny, but across a span hundreds of feet long, it adds up. A steel bridge deck in a climate with hot summers and cold winters can change length by several inches over a year. If both ends were fixed solidly in place, that movement would have nowhere to go, and the stress would crack the concrete, buckle the deck or push against the supports.

Expansion joints give the movement a place to happen. Some joints are simple, with interlocking steel teeth that slide past one another like the fingers of two hands. Larger bridges use joints made of several rubber seals and steel beams that can open and close over a wide range. Under the deck, bearings let the bridge slide slightly on its supports instead of dragging them along.

The joints solve one problem but create another. Because they are gaps, they let water, road salt and grit fall onto the structure below, where they can rust steel and weaken concrete. Joints also wear out faster than most other parts of a bridge, and replacing one usually means closing lanes. For this reason, engineers designing shorter bridges increasingly try to avoid joints entirely, building the deck and supports as one flexible unit that bends slightly as temperatures change.

Whether engineers add joints or design around them, the goal is the same: a structure that accepts the fact that its materials move. A bridge stays standing not by resisting every force but by allowing for the ones it cannot stop.$q$, 'draft')
